Seven classes compose IO::K8s::APIObject that upstream has no GVK for, so
TO_JSON stamps apiVersion and kind into an embedded field that has no such
keys in the Kubernetes schema. The most-used one is PodTemplateSpec, which
means every Deployment, StatefulSet, DaemonSet, ReplicaSet, Job and CronJob
this distribution serializes carries the extra pair.

Measured on 4887f658:

  $k8s->struct_to_object('Api::Apps::V1::Deployment', {...})->TO_JSON
  {
     "apiVersion" : "apps/v1",
     "kind" : "Deployment",
     "spec" : {
        "template" : {
           "apiVersion" : "v1",            <-- not in the schema
           "kind" : "PodTemplateSpec",     <-- not in the schema
           "metadata" : {...}, "spec" : {...}
        }
     }
  }

Checked against spec/v1.36.3.json, definitions block:

  io.k8s.api.core.v1.PodTemplateSpec        no GVK, props: metadata,spec
  io.k8s.api.batch.v1.JobTemplateSpec       no GVK, props: metadata,spec
  io.k8s.api.core.v1.PersistentVolumeClaimTemplate  no GVK, props: metadata,spec
  io.k8s.api.resource.v1.ResourceClaimTemplateSpec  no GVK, props: metadata,spec

  # controls, for contrast -- these DO carry apiVersion/kind upstream and
  # are correct as they are:
  io.k8s.api.core.v1.PodTemplate            GVK, props: apiVersion,kind,metadata,template
  io.k8s.api.core.v1.PersistentVolumeClaim  GVK, props: apiVersion,kind,metadata,spec,status
  io.k8s.api.autoscaling.v1.Scale           GVK
  io.k8s.api.policy.v1.Eviction             GVK
  io.k8s.api.authentication.v1.TokenRequest GVK

THE SEVEN CLASSES (all `use IO::K8s::APIObject;`, all should not)

  lib/IO/K8s/Api/Core/V1/PodTemplateSpec.pm              embedded 7x
  lib/IO/K8s/Api/Batch/V1/JobTemplateSpec.pm             embedded 1x
  lib/IO/K8s/Api/Core/V1/PersistentVolumeClaimTemplate.pm  embedded 1x
  lib/IO/K8s/Api/Resource/V1/ResourceClaimTemplateSpec.pm       embedded 1x
  lib/IO/K8s/Api/Resource/V1alpha3/ResourceClaimTemplateSpec.pm embedded 1x
  lib/IO/K8s/Api/Resource/V1beta1/ResourceClaimTemplateSpec.pm  embedded 1x
  lib/IO/K8s/Api/Resource/V1beta2/ResourceClaimTemplateSpec.pm  embedded 1x

Embedding sites for PodTemplateSpec, found by walking the attribute
registry for is_object/is_array_of_objects/is_hash_of_objects targets whose
class reports _is_resource:

  Api::Apps::V1::DaemonSetSpec.template
  Api::Apps::V1::DeploymentSpec.template
  Api::Apps::V1::ReplicaSetSpec.template
  Api::Apps::V1::StatefulSetSpec.template
  Api::Batch::V1::JobSpec.template
  Api::Core::V1::PodTemplate.template
  Api::Core::V1::ReplicationControllerSpec.template
  (JobTemplateSpec: Batch::V1::CronJobSpec.jobTemplate;
   PersistentVolumeClaimTemplate: Core::V1::EphemeralVolumeSource.volumeClaimTemplate;
   ResourceClaimTemplateSpec: Resource::V*::ResourceClaimTemplate.spec)

The same scan finds exactly one other embedded class that emits the pair --
Core::V1::PersistentVolumeClaim, in StatefulSetSpec.volumeClaimTemplates and
three AgentSandbox specs. That one is CORRECT: PVC really does carry inline
TypeMeta upstream. Do not "fix" it.

WHY IT MATTERS
kubectl's default field validation is strict, and the API server rejects
unknown fields under fieldValidation=Strict rather than pruning them, so
`kubectl apply -f` on to_yaml() output should fail with an unknown-field
error on spec.template.apiVersion. NOT verified against a live cluster --
verify that before writing the Changes entry, and say which way it went. In
the lenient path the fields are pruned and the manifest merely carries
noise.

The distribution's own POD already states the rule this breaks --
lib/IO/K8s/APIObject.pm:61: "Use C<IO::K8s::Resource> for embedded objects
(PodSpec, Container, etc.)". These seven are embedded objects using the
wrong base.

WHY NO TEST CAUGHT IT
t/25_real_world.t inflates real manifests and re-exports them, but every
assertion is a per-field spot check; its 12 is_deeply calls all compare leaf
arrays (policyTypes, verbs, hosts, ...), never a whole manifest against its
input. t/26_build_verify.t has no is_deeply at all. So nothing in the suite
notices seven extra keys. maint/spec-drift-check.pl does not see it either
-- apiVersion/kind come from the role, not from the k8s() registry it reads,
so its EXTRA FIELD tier reports 0. (Separate ticket for that blind spot.)

WHAT TO DO -- design decision first, this is not a mechanical swap
The classes genuinely need `metadata` (ObjectMeta), which today comes from
IO::K8s::Role::APIObject together with the TypeMeta emission and the
convenience methods. Options, pick one and say why:
  (a) `use IO::K8s::Resource;` plus an explicit
      `k8s metadata => 'Apimachinery::Pkg::Apis::Meta::V1::ObjectMeta';`
      -- smallest mechanism, but drops the label/annotation helpers from
      these classes, which is a visible API removal for consumers who call
      $deployment->spec->template->add_label(...). CHECK whether that is
      used anywhere before choosing it.
  (b) an embedded marker (e.g. `_is_resource` returning 0, or an import
      parameter) that keeps metadata and the helpers but stops TO_JSON
      writing apiVersion/kind. Role::Resource::TO_JSON:100 already gates the
      TypeMeta block on `$self->can('_is_resource') && $self->_is_resource`,
      so the hook exists.
  (b) is the smaller behaviour change; (a) is the more honest model. The
  resource_plural POD (Role/APIObject.pm:546-549) already calls these "the
  embedded PodTemplateSpec-style types [that] never appear as a kind: on the
  wire at all" -- the model already knows; only TO_JSON does not.

Removing api_version()/kind() from a public class is a breaking change under
the house protocol; state the changed failure mode in Changes either way.

REGRESSION TEST (the actual point)
Add a structural check, not another spot check: inflate a manifest and
assert the re-exported structure has NO key the input did not have, at
least for one Deployment and one CronJob. That is the assertion class that
was missing, and it is what keeps this from coming back.

Lane: io-k8s-worker for lib/, io-k8s-test-writer for the structural test.

Cross-reference: the checker blind spot that let this through is karr #50.
The structural round-trip assertion this ticket asks for would also have
caught it -- see the WHY NO TEST CAUGHT IT section.
Design entschieden: Option (a).
